        "Large Language Models (LLMs) built upon the autoregressive Transformer architecture have fundamentally reshaped automated question answering, abstractive document summarization, clinical and legal decision support, software synthesis, and multi-turn conversational systems [3, 18]. By scaling self-supervised next-token prediction across trillions of tokens and aligning model behavior via instruction tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback, modern foundation models achieve remarkable linguistic fluency and broad cross-domain generalization. However, the deployment of generative language models in high-stakes operational environments remains severely constrained by their propensity to hallucinate—generating statements that are grammatically coherent, rhetorically confident, and contextually plausible, yet factually false with respect to world knowledge or directly contradictory to the provided source context [22, 35].",
